WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.140
This started when my friend Marc LeBrun, who lives in California,

00:00:05.109 --> 00:00:11.000
he's a computer scientist. He likes to consider unusual ways of doing arithmetic,

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.000
and he came up with this idea.

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:18.500
You know that in school, children have a lot of trouble when they learn arithmetic.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.939
There's all this trouble with the carrys;

00:00:21.939 --> 00:00:23.939
it's very complicated.

00:00:24.039 --> 00:00:26.039
So he said, let- let's make it

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:29.500
simple for the kiddies. We're not gonna have any carrys.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.800
We're gonna abolish all carrys.

00:00:31.869 --> 00:00:34.139
We're gonna change the rules, a bit.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:40.900
Now when you add two digits, two numbers like 7 and 8, the result is not 15 anymore.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:45.450
On the moon, it's 8. You pick the bigger of the two numbers.

00:00:47.829 --> 00:00:52.700
So 1 plus 1 is 1, sure. 1 + 2 is 2.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:54.700
When you multiply, you pick the smaller.

00:00:55.629 --> 00:00:57.959
So 1 times 2 is 1.

00:00:58.629 --> 00:01:01.300
Alright, and that's it. Those are the rules,

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.059
that's lunar arithmetic.

00:01:03.059 --> 00:01:05.000
(Brady: So 3 times 8 is 3?)

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:09.000
3 times 8 is 3. 3 plus 8 is 8,

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:11.250
you take the bigger of the two.

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:14.880
(Brady: I hope they didn't actually use this on the moon.)

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:15.700
Maybe they didn't, maybe they will

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.200
in future.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.200
(Brady: Okay, I understand the rules, I'm a bit worried)

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.340
(about what's gonna happen here, but-)

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:22.530
Let's do some arithmetic.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:28.409
Let's do an addition, for example. Suppose we wanted to add, say, 58 and

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:34.700
19. What you do is you say, 8 plus 9 is 9.

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:36.700
Remember, you take the bigger of the two.

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:42.479
And there are no carrys, 8 plus 9 is 9, 1 plus 5 is

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:47.700
5. You take the bigger of the two, bigger of 1 and 5 is 5.

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:52.560
The biggest of 9 and 8 is 9. So 58 plus 19 is

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:54.939
equal to 59.

00:01:54.939 --> 00:01:56.079
That's

00:01:56.079 --> 00:01:59.909
lunar addition. What about multiplication? Suppose we want to multiply,

00:02:00.759 --> 00:02:03.869
say, 17 by 24.

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:08.459
Remember, digit by digit. So four sevens are?

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:13.939
(Brady: 4)
- 4, very good. You pick the smaller. Four 1s are

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:22.670
1. And now two 7s. Two 7s are 2, two 1s are 1. And now we add them up. 1,

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:25.130
1 plus 2 is

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:32.360
2 - you pick the bigger of the two. And you bring down the 4. So 17 times

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:37.240
24 is equal to 124 on the moon.

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:44.860
So now you can do a lot of arithmetic in lunar arithmetic, and you can produce a lot of interesting number sequences.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:55.699
[Apollo mission recording snippets]

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:04.680
What are the squares in lunar arithmetic?

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:06.820
What's 1 times 1? It's 1.

00:03:07.739 --> 00:03:09.739
2 times 2

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:13.339
is equal to - pick the smaller - it's 2.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:15.989
3 times 3,

00:03:15.989 --> 00:03:23.899
3 squared, is 3. 6 squared is 6, 7 squared is 7, 8 squared is 8, 9 squared is 9 - what about 10

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:30.860
squared? 10 times 10 - we have to do it. Zero times zero, you pick the smaller. 0 times 1 is 0.

00:03:31.049 --> 00:03:35.239
1 times 0 is 0, 1 times 1 is 1, you add them up. 1, 0,

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:41.120
plus 0 - pick the bigger - and we get 100, just like back on earth! But what about 11 squared?

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:41.600
Well

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:48.469
we have to do it. 1 1 times 1 1, we get 1 1, 1 1 - add them up. 1, 1 plus 1 is 1, 1.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:52.730
So 11 squared is 1 1 1 - 111.

00:03:54.989 --> 00:03:59.959
12 squared: 2 times 2 is is 2, 2 times 1 is 1, 1 times 2 is

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:06.860
1. 1 times 1 is 1 - always pick the smaller. And we add them up 1, 1 plus 1 is

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:10.250
1, 2 is 2; so 12 squared is

00:04:10.799 --> 00:04:12.359
112.

00:04:12.359 --> 00:04:13.890
And

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:16.970
so on. 19 squared is

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:23.450
119, and so on. So that's the sequence of lunar squares.

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:28.080
[Apollo mission recordings]

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:34.320
If you are interested in the formal details,

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:36.669
this is an associative, commutative,

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:40.029
distributive law - so it works.

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:42.280
We don't have subtraction,

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:49.000
we don't have division, but apart from that we can do anything. What I would like to tell you about next are the lunar primes.

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:54.939
What are the prime numbers on the moon? You're really gonna like this - and I need more paper.

00:04:54.939 --> 00:04:56.939
(Brady: I'll get you some)

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:02.320
What are the lunar primes? Well

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:06.670
what is a prime? First of all we have to decide what it means for a number to be a prime.

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:08.860
Now you remember back on Earth,

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:13.719
7 was a prime because you couldn't write 7 as 2 times 3,

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:20.679
or anything except 1 times 7. If the only way to factorise it is as 1 times itself

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:22.779
then it's a prime number. On the moon

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:27.279
is 7 a prime, on the moon? Well, notice

00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:30.729
7 is equal to 8 times 7,

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:32.870
right?

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:35.320
Because 8 times 7 - you pick the smaller of the two,

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:39.039
which is 7. So 7 is 8 times 7, so 7 is not a prime!

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:42.220
Because it has a factorisation.

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:44.800
But really before we get down

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:51.039
to the details of this, we have to decide what precisely it means to be a prime. So

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:58.960
formerly a number's a prime if the only factorisation is 1 times itself. What does 1 mean on the moon?

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:06.370
We have to say what is 1 - if I put it in quotes - on the moon?

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:12.219
Well what is 1? 1 has the property that if you multiply anything by 1 you get what you started with.

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:18.549
1 times 3 is 3, 1 times 111 is 111, right. On the moon that's not true.

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:22.960
1 times 3 on the moon is not 3, it's 1. 1 times;

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:27.999
well 1 times 111 happens to be 111 by accident.

00:06:28.910 --> 00:06:31.929
1 on the moon - 1 times anything is

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:35.619
basically all 1s and zeros. 1 times 30

00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:37.070
is

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:38.330
ten,

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:42.940
because you go through a digit by digit and pick the smaller. So 1 on the moon is not 1.

00:06:43.850 --> 00:06:46.839
What is 1 on the moon? Would you care to make a guess?

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:49.899
What's the number? 
- (Brady: Is it-)

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:54.340
(I haven't thought about it, is it 9?)
- It's 9! Very good. Fantastic, brilliant.

00:06:54.340 --> 00:07:01.090
Because when you multiply something by 9, it just copies what it sees. 9 times 7 is 7,

00:07:01.090 --> 00:07:04.810
9 times 1 is 1 - it just copies what it sees. So on the moon

00:07:05.510 --> 00:07:07.510
9 is the 1.

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:11.260
So on the moon a prime number

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:17.229
has the property that the only way you can factorise it is 9 times itself.

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:20.380
So 7 is

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:23.709
obviously not a prime, because seven is equal to 8 times 7.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:30.430
What about 8? 8 is not a prime because 8 is equal to 8 times 8, so it's not a prime.

00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:36.519
It has a factorisation in which one of the fact- neither factor is 9. Any number if- for it to be a prime

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:44.109
the biggest digit has to be a 9. So 25 is not a prime because we could multiply it by 5

00:07:44.390 --> 00:07:46.390
and get what we started with.

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:52.540
So we've gotta have a 9 in the number. So 19 is the smallest.

00:07:53.150 --> 00:07:55.150
We must have a 9.

00:07:55.240 --> 00:07:57.240
(Brady: There's no way to build)

00:07:57.290 --> 00:08:04.389
(19 except, what, 19-)
- Times 9. 
- (Right)
- You can't factorise because it's got a 9 in it.

00:08:04.390 --> 00:08:11.050
So if, if it was 18, we can multiply 18 by an 8 and get 18. We'd have a factorisation.

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:16.000
So you can always multiply by the biggest digit and get what you started with.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:22.809
So the big- biggest digit had better be 9. 
(Brady: These primes are going to be rare then?)
- Yes, but not all that rare.

00:08:22.850 --> 00:08:24.290
So yeah

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:32.259
how rare are they? Well, let's look at the prime numbers. So what about 9 itself? So  back on Earth

00:08:32.810 --> 00:08:39.099
we don't say 1 is a prime, because it's - we wouldn't have unique factorisation. 2 is the smallest prime on Earth.

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:47.110
But on the moon, just as 1 isn't a prime on on Earth, 9 isn't a prime. But apart from that

00:08:48.830 --> 00:08:53.139
we get 19. So the primes, the Lunar primes,

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:56.450
19, 29,

00:08:56.450 --> 00:08:58.450
39, 49,

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:00.669
59, 69, 79, 89,

00:09:01.670 --> 00:09:03.670
90 is a prime,

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:08.619
91. As long as there's a 9 and there's no obvious way to factorise it;

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:14.739
you have to check that 90 is a prime. We can't get 90 by multiplying two numbers.

00:09:14.780 --> 00:09:22.389
You have to check it, but it's true. It's easy to check. 99, we get 109, yes, that's a prime. But

00:09:22.940 --> 00:09:27.249
119 is not because, as we saw a moment ago,

00:09:28.010 --> 00:09:30.010
19 times 19

00:09:30.050 --> 00:09:32.050
is

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:39.789
119. So 119 is not a prime. But roughly speaking most members that have a 9 in them are primes.

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:47.050
So those are the lunar primes. If we were to go to the OEIS and we, in the search window we type lunar

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:54.820
primes - we used to call them dismal primes because teaching of arithmetic in schools were so dismal. It's a dismal definition,

00:09:54.820 --> 00:10:01.809
I think we like lunar primes much better. So, well, I can show you the graph - or two graphs. One of the first 200 terms,

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:05.439
technically it's called a pin plot because we draw little pins.

00:10:05.780 --> 00:10:08.259
You don't see the pins there because they're quite dense.

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:15.459
Then you get a scatterplot, just a plain graph of however many terms we have, and it looks like we're getting about;

00:10:15.710 --> 00:10:20.199
what is it, 2500 terms. There's one more thing I would like to show you.

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:22.749
I'd like to prove that they're infinitely many primes.

00:10:23.780 --> 00:10:25.780
You remember-
- (Brady: Lunar primes?)

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:28.060
Infinitely many lunar primes!

00:10:28.130 --> 00:10:34.210
First of all I'll prove that 109 is prime. And the proof of that is: suppose it wasn't,

00:10:34.760 --> 00:10:38.619
it would have to be the product of two two-digit numbers.

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:41.410
They would have to be two digit numbers because of the rules.

00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:45.850
So it would have to be ab times cd. But we've got to

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:48.400
get a one at the beginning.

00:10:48.830 --> 00:10:50.830
So one of a and c,

00:10:51.380 --> 00:10:54.400
remember how it works, the table - the multiplication,

00:10:54.950 --> 00:11:00.280
the tableau. If you get 1 at the beginning either a or c has to be a

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:05.320
1. And to get a 9 at the end, both b and d have to be 9s.

00:11:05.570 --> 00:11:08.409
That's the other, only possibility because when we do the multiplication

00:11:08.959 --> 00:11:15.789
9 times 9 is 9. 9 times 1, well, that's, that's 1 - pick the smaller of the two. Okay,

00:11:15.790 --> 00:11:18.159
now we do the c. c times 9 is,

00:11:18.740 --> 00:11:21.459
well, it's c. Whatever C is.

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:25.959
Multiplying it by 9 doesn't change it. And c times 1,

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:33.940
well c can't be 0 so it's gonna be 1. Now we add them up and we get 1, 1 plus c. I

00:11:34.610 --> 00:11:40.149
don't know what it is. It's the bigger of 1 and c. Well, it's gonna be c.

00:11:40.190 --> 00:11:46.659
It could be 1 - anyway what we get is the number 1c9. But c can't be 0,

00:11:47.990 --> 00:11:51.250
because if c was 0 we wouldn't get the 1 in front.

00:11:52.370 --> 00:11:58.630
So this can't happen. This is a contradiction. If c was equal to 0 we wouldn't get the 1 in front.

00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:04.580
We cannot factorise 109 as a product of two primes. And then, likewise,

00:12:04.580 --> 00:12:10.560
1 0 0 0 0 9 is prime by the same argument.

00:12:15.290 --> 00:12:17.290
There are infinitely many primes.

00:12:20.449 --> 00:12:24.729
(Brady: Yeah, no matter how many, you can put as many zeros there as you want?)
- As many zeros as you want and you get a prime.

00:12:26.980 --> 00:12:31.440
(Brady: Nice proof!)
- Yeah. Yeah. Nice theorem. Lunar primes, lunar arithmetic.

